
## Income and earnings

1. tiền dùng cho hđ khác ngoài basic living costs
  - Such amount of money is only enough for me to meet **basic living costs** (housing and food), leaving virtually no **leaving virtually no disposable income**.
2. salary after tax and reduction
  - My **take-home pay** is decent, but inflation has reduced what it can buy.
3. to have just enough money to live
  - When I was a student, I was basically trying to **make ends meet**.
4. nguồn thu nhập ổn định
  - **Freelancing** is flexible, but a **steady income stream** of a 9-to-5 job is more **reassuring**.
5. to earn extra money beside your income
  - Some people **take on weekend work** to **supplement their income**.

## Spend and save money

1. chỉ chi tiêu trong khả năng của bản thân, không dùng tín dụng -> nhịn ăn khi giá cao
  - I try to **live within my means** instead of **relying on credit**. So whenever prices when up, I have to **tighten my belts** for that month.
2. mua sắm không theo kế hoạch
  - Online shopping makes **impulse purchases** dangerously easy.
3. vung tiền
  - I rarely **splurge**, but I’ll **pay extra** for my necessities such as laptop for my study.
4. theo dõi chi tiêu
  - **Tracking my expenses** helps me figure out which make up most of my expenses.
5. tiết kiệm tiền
  - I **put a little money aside** each month for emergencies.
6. chi tiêu có kế hoạch
  - Nowadays it's hard to **stick to a budget** because more and more companies try to **stimulate your impulse purchases**.
7. sống qua ngày bằng lương tháng
  - A lot of families live **paycheck to paycheck**, so one **emergency** can be devastating.
8. chi tiêu quá mức
  - **Allocating** a fixed amount of money for saving every month prevents me from **overspending repulsively**.

## Banking, loan and debt

1. mượn nợ
  - Many graduates have to **take out a loan** for their study.
2. bị thiếu nợ
  - The credit card interest can leave people **debt-ridden** surprisingly quickly.
3. hoàn thành việc trả nợ
  - I’m trying to **pay off** my student loan **within** three years.
4. đặt cọc
  - You **pay a deposit upfront**, then make monthly **installments** for three years until you fully own the house.

## Investment and making money

1. collection of investments
  - A **balanced portfolio** usually includes different asset types.
2. spread investments to reduce risk
  - **Diversifying** helps protect you when one market drops.
3. trong thời gian dài hạn
  - With a **long-term horizon**, **short-term volatility** is less stressful.

## Prices, inflation and cost of living

1. khả năng mua
  - Even with the same salary, **inflation** makes your **purchasing power** shrink over time.
2. chi phí ăn ở
  - The **cost of living** has **climbed**, so people are **cutting back**.
3. tăng giá
  - There was a **price hike** in petrol, and transport costs **went up**, too.
4. cost-effective: đáng tiền

## Social issues

1. chênh lệch thu nhập giữa người thu nhập cao và người thu nhập thấp
  - **Income disparity** can lead to **social tension** and reduced **mobility**.
2. khả năng thay đổi tầng lớp trong xã hội (khả năng làm giàu, tiến thân)
  - Education can improve **social mobility**, but only if it’s **affordable**.
3. dễ bị ảnh hưởng về mặt tài chính
  - People without savings are **financially vulnerable** to unexpected bills.
4. sự hỗ trợ (từ gia đình / chính phủ) về mặt tài chính
  - A stronger **welfare safety net** can reduce extreme **poverty**.

## The economy

1. kiến thức về tài chính và quản lý chi tiêu
  - **Financial literacy** should be taught at school so people avoid toxic debt.
2. sự ảm đạm (đi xuống) của nền kinh tế -> công ty ngừng tuyển nhân lực
  - During an **economic downturn**, companies often **freeze hiring**.
3. government's policy about taxes
  - The government's **fiscal policy** can affects the economy greatly, for example cutting taxes can have the effect of stimulating the economy.
